### Describe the bug

In the `cancel_reward function`, there is an inconsistency with Solidity regarding the authorization check.
In the documentation repository, there is the Solidity implementation of TIP20.

```
function cancelReward(uint64 id) external returns (uint256 refund) {
    ......
    // Attempt to transfer refund from pool to funder (TIP-403 check)
    if (
        TIP403_REGISTRY.isAuthorized(transferPolicyId, address(this))
            && TIP403_REGISTRY.isAuthorized(transferPolicyId, s.funder)
    ) {
        ......
    }
}
```

In Solidity's cancelReward, both the contract address and the funder address are checked to see if they are on the blacklist or whitelist, and this determines whether the transaction is permitted.
However, in Rust, `crates/precompiles/src/tip20/rewards.rs`, only the funder address is checked against the blacklist or whitelist:

```
pub fn cancel_reward(
        &mut self,
        msg_sender: Address,
        call: ITIP20::cancelRewardCall,
    ) -> Result<U256> {
    ......
    if refund > U256::ZERO && self.is_transfer_authorized(stream.funder, stream.funder)? {
        ......
    }    
}
```
This discrepancy may cause the blacklist or whitelist to fail in certain scenarios, leading to unexpected transactions.
